    {
      "title": "Putin Orders Pause in Kyiv Strikes Ahead of Visit by U.S. Envoys",
      "link": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/05/world/europe/russia-ukraine-witkoff-kushner-moscow.html",
      "description": "Steve Witkoff, President Trump’s special envoy, and Jared Kushner, his son-in-law, arrived in Moscow to meet with the Russian leader. They plan to visit Kyiv next, in an effort to revive peace talks.",
      "source_name": "NYT World News",
      "published": "2026-09-05T13:49:50"
    },
    {
      "title": "Putin pauses strikes on Kyiv for 72 hours as US envoys visit Russia",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/putin-orders-72-hour-pause-in-kyiv-strikes-as-us-envoys-seek-peace-126090500774_1.html",
      "description": "Russian President Vladimir Putin ordered a 72-hour pause in strikes on Kyiv, the Kremlin said Saturday, as US envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner arrived in Moscow to try to revive a stalled push to end Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine.\n\"Putin has ordered that no strikes be carried out against Kyiv for three days, starting at midnight today,\" presidential spokesperson Dmitry Peskov told Russia's Tass news agency. He said the decision was tied to preparations for Witkoff and Kushner's planned visit to the Ukrainian capital, which Kyiv has said will take place on Sunday.\nThe envoys' trip comes as both sides escalate aerial attacks, some 4.5-years after the Kremlin's invasion of its neighbour. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said Friday that Ukraine would halt airstrikes during the envoys' visit and urged Moscow to do the same.\nWashington's push to end the fighting, now in its fifth year, has lost momentum as US attention has been focused for the last six months on it",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-05T13:00:45"
    },
    {
      "title": "Witkoff, Kushner arrive in Moscow to revive stalled Ukraine peace push",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/witkoff-kushner-arrive-in-moscow-to-revive-ukraine-peace-talks-126090500506_1.html",
      "description": "US envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner arrived in Moscow on Saturday to try to revive a stalled push to end Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine, Russian state news outlets reported.\nThe visit came as both sides escalated their aerial attacks.\nThe envoys were met at the airport by Russian envoy Kirill Dmitriev, Russia's state news agency Tass said.\nThe last known visit to Moscow by Witkoff and Kushner, who is President Donald Trump's son-in-law, was in January 2026, when they met Russian President Vladimir Putin in the Kremlin. They have never visited Kyiv, but President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said he was expecting them in the Ukrainian capital on Sunday.\nWashington's push to end the fighting, now in its fifth year, has lost momentum as US attention has been focused for the last six months on its war with Iran.\nEarlier in the week, Putin had described negotiations as \"to a certain extent frozen.",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-05T10:57:52"
    },
